WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.820 --> 00:00:07.021
The Jackass family was never the same after
the death of Ryan Dunn, who died aged 34 in

00:00:07.021 --> 00:00:09.059
a devastating car crash.

00:00:09.059 --> 00:00:13.679
His life til then was an inspiration, but
it was also filled with trouble, and signs

00:00:13.679 --> 00:00:14.999
of the tragedy to come.

00:00:15.999 --> 00:00:20.380
Born in 1977, Ryan Dunn moved multiple times
over the course of his childhood.

00:00:20.380 --> 00:00:24.580
He began his life in Ohio, went to New York,
and finally ended up in Pennsylvania.

00:00:24.580 --> 00:00:28.500
It was there that Dunn attended high school
and met Bam Margera.

00:00:28.500 --> 00:00:32.610
The two became fast friends and remained close
throughout Dunn's life.

00:00:32.610 --> 00:00:37.520
Soon after graduation, Dunn snagged his first
acting role, playing a soldier in the 1996

00:00:37.520 --> 00:00:38.650
sci-fi movie "Lifeform."

00:00:38.650 --> 00:00:43.620
However, it wasn't until 1999 that he began
to do what made him famous.

00:00:43.620 --> 00:00:47.430
That's when he began working with Margera
on the "CKY" videos that would become the

00:00:47.430 --> 00:00:50.010
foundation out of which "Jackass" grew.

00:00:50.010 --> 00:00:55.160
"Jackass" itself arrived on MTV in 2000, and
Dunn was there from the start.

00:00:55.160 --> 00:01:00.510
He appeared in all 25 episodes of the series
and then went on to do stunts in "Jackass:

00:01:00.510 --> 00:01:04.300
The Movie," "Jackass Number Two," and "Jackass
3D."

00:01:04.300 --> 00:01:08.500
He appeared in other projects with members
of the Jackass crew, including Steve-O and,

00:01:08.500 --> 00:01:11.390
most prominently, his friend Bam Margera.

00:01:11.390 --> 00:01:16.730
He acted as well, but not as often and typically
in small or independent projects that got

00:01:16.730 --> 00:01:18.909
less attention than his "Jackass" work.

00:01:20.150 --> 00:01:23.920
In 2005, police pulled over Dunn while he
was operating a motor vehicle.

00:01:23.920 --> 00:01:28.479
They deduced his level of intoxication at
the scene, and eventually, the town charged

00:01:28.479 --> 00:01:29.640
him with a DUI.

00:01:29.640 --> 00:01:34.070
In his late 20s at the time, this was his
first recorded incident of driving under the

00:01:34.070 --> 00:01:35.640
effects of a substance.

00:01:35.640 --> 00:01:39.830
Given that, the court determined Dunn was
an appropriate candidate for the first-time

00:01:39.830 --> 00:01:43.210
offender's program, an opportunity which he
pursued.

00:01:43.210 --> 00:01:48.900
This pre-trial diversion program would typically
allow a motorist facing charges to avoid jail

00:01:48.900 --> 00:01:54.479
time, receive reduced license suspension time,
or have the charges eliminated entirely and

00:01:54.479 --> 00:01:56.840
eventually have their record cleared.

00:01:56.840 --> 00:01:59.390
Dunn completed the program as prescribed.

00:01:59.390 --> 00:02:03.979
He still did have his license suspended for
over a year, which is around the standard

00:02:03.979 --> 00:02:06.770
for a first time DUI offense in Pennsylvania.

00:02:06.770 --> 00:02:11.050
However, the offenders' program ensured the
charges against him were dropped after he

00:02:11.050 --> 00:02:12.660
completed the probationary period.

00:02:13.680 --> 00:02:19.390
In addition to his 2005 DUI, Ryan Dunn had
quite a few problems when it came to sitting

00:02:19.390 --> 00:02:20.660
behind the wheel.

00:02:20.660 --> 00:02:25.940
As noted in a Philadelphia Inquirer piece
from 2011, the stunt performer had a reputation

00:02:25.940 --> 00:02:27.740
when it came to driving.

00:02:27.740 --> 00:02:30.190
"Pulled over."

00:02:30.190 --> 00:02:32.630
"How fast?"

00:02:32.630 --> 00:02:36.310
"167, was it?"

00:02:36.310 --> 00:02:41.830
The "Jackass" star was known for loving speed
and adrenaline while in a car and had a tendency

00:02:41.830 --> 00:02:43.520
to be relaxed about safety.

00:02:43.520 --> 00:02:48.540
In "Jackass" and participating in Gumball
3000 events — international races that took

00:02:48.540 --> 00:02:53.010
place on public roads and frequently attracted
celebrity participants — he found lucrative

00:02:53.010 --> 00:02:56.190
and somewhat approved ways to express himself.

00:02:56.190 --> 00:03:00.640
Unfortunately, he often didn't turn off the
need for speed when it came to getting around

00:03:00.640 --> 00:03:01.950
in his day-to-day life.

00:03:01.950 --> 00:03:05.250
This approach to driving didn't escape law
enforcement's notice.

00:03:05.250 --> 00:03:12.500
In the 13 years from 1998 to 2011, Dunn wracked
up 23 violations.

00:03:12.500 --> 00:03:16.760
Three of those times, he was pulled over and
charged for driving with a suspended license,

00:03:16.760 --> 00:03:20.950
and 10 stops earned him tickets for speeding
and reckless driving.

00:03:20.950 --> 00:03:25.470
After Dunn's death, Bam Margera's mother,
April, recalled constantly warning him about

00:03:25.470 --> 00:03:26.730
his driving habits.

00:03:26.730 --> 00:03:27.970
"He drove too fast.

00:03:27.970 --> 00:03:30.260
I yelled at him all the time about that."

00:03:31.260 --> 00:03:35.251
In the closing stunt of "Jackass Number Two,"
the plan was for a horse to pull Ryan Dunn

00:03:35.251 --> 00:03:38.110
and Bam Margera off their feet and then out
of view.

00:03:38.110 --> 00:03:44.510
Visually, the scene went as planned, but the
physical consequences for Dunn were anything but routine.

00:03:44.510 --> 00:03:47.280
When Dunn went to the ground, he landed on
his shoulder.

00:03:47.280 --> 00:03:52.720
His muscles in that area took the full force
of the impact, an immediately painful consequence.

00:03:52.720 --> 00:03:57.760
However, the repercussions of the fall proved
worse than the typical kinds of bruises and

00:03:57.760 --> 00:04:00.640
breaks the Jackass gang had grown to accept.

00:04:00.640 --> 00:04:06.310
The damage from the fall was serious enough
to cause a blood clot, which was potentially life-threatening.

00:04:06.310 --> 00:04:11.450
As "Jackass Number Two" director Jeff Tremaine
explained in an MTV tribute to Dunn, the damage

00:04:11.450 --> 00:04:15.470
was much more serious and lasting than it
may have looked on screen.

00:04:15.470 --> 00:04:19.260
"A blood clot is really dangerous because
if a chunk of that went into his heart or

00:04:19.260 --> 00:04:21.100
his brain, he would die."

00:04:21.100 --> 00:04:27.340
In the same MTV tribute, friend Cameron Taylor
said Dunn was actually quite frightened, explaining

00:04:27.340 --> 00:04:32.510
he'd "never seen a man more scared" and that
Dunn could've died "at any minute."

00:04:32.510 --> 00:04:36.930
Fortunately, Dunn received treatment that
saved his life, but things were about to get

00:04:36.930 --> 00:04:38.300
even worse for the stuntman.

00:04:39.440 --> 00:04:41.790
The hits kept coming for Dunn.

00:04:41.790 --> 00:04:46.410
While receiving treatment for his blood clot,
Dunn was diagnosed with Lyme disease.

00:04:46.410 --> 00:04:51.350
In an interview with Film Threat in 2010,
Dunn confirmed the diagnosis and his recovery,

00:04:51.350 --> 00:04:55.820
although he didn't offer any insight into
when or where he came into contact with the

00:04:55.820 --> 00:04:58.470
infected tick that passed him the virus.

00:04:58.470 --> 00:05:04.040
While his treatment for Lyme proved successful,
the combination of the blood clot, the disease,

00:05:04.040 --> 00:05:07.510
and the long recovery process seemed to add
up.

00:05:07.510 --> 00:05:12.080
In the same interview where he revealed his
history with Lyme, Dunn attempted to downplay

00:05:12.080 --> 00:05:16.210
it as just taking a break after 10 years of
work.

00:05:16.210 --> 00:05:20.430
Subsequent publications, however, have confirmed
depression was an ongoing struggle for the

00:05:20.430 --> 00:05:21.760
actor and stunt performer.

00:05:21.760 --> 00:05:26.200
In fact, he slipped into such a significant
depression that he cut himself off from contact

00:05:26.200 --> 00:05:29.970
with many of his friends, including the Jackass
crew, for two years.

00:05:31.380 --> 00:05:36.580
Despite the way it came about, the hiatus
from performing seemed to do Dunn some good.

00:05:36.580 --> 00:05:41.770
He returned to the world of reckless and hilarious
stunts in "Jackass 3D" and appeared happy

00:05:41.770 --> 00:05:42.870
to be back.

00:05:42.870 --> 00:05:47.020
He referred to the experience as his favorite
on-screen work.

00:05:47.020 --> 00:05:51.210
Experiencing early apprehension about returning
only to find the same camaraderie he always

00:05:51.210 --> 00:05:56.462
had with the team came as a relief and led
to the most fun he had making a "Jackass" film.

00:05:56.462 --> 00:06:01.280
He booked three acting jobs in the same year,
and also produced his first film, "Living

00:06:01.280 --> 00:06:04.090
Will," in which he was also cast as the lead
role.

00:06:04.090 --> 00:06:07.910
"I'm eating everything I can until I poop."

00:06:07.910 --> 00:06:12.490
He even struck off on his own to co-host a
show on G4 with Jessica Chobot, his first

00:06:12.490 --> 00:06:15.850
prominent TV work without any members of the
Jackass crew.

00:06:15.850 --> 00:06:21.560
The show, "Proving Ground," involved attempting
to replicate pop culture experiences in real life.

00:06:21.560 --> 00:06:25.220
For instance, an early episode tried to realize
the Mario Kart experience.

00:06:25.220 --> 00:06:29.941
Given Dunn's established stunt bona fides
and his prominent place in pop culture, it

00:06:29.941 --> 00:06:31.710
seemed a promising fit.

00:06:31.710 --> 00:06:35.426
For the first time in years, Dunn's career
prospects seemed on-track.

00:06:36.270 --> 00:06:41.389
On June 19, 2011, Dunn was back in his hometown
and celebrating.

00:06:41.389 --> 00:06:45.800
Joining him for a night on the town was Zachary
Hartwell, a longtime friend who also worked

00:06:45.800 --> 00:06:48.680
as a production assistant on "Jackass Number
Two."

00:06:48.680 --> 00:06:52.680
Sometime around 2:30 the next morning, the
two decided to call it quits.

00:06:52.680 --> 00:06:57.620
But as Dunn was driving — with Hartwell
in the car — he soon hit speeds in excess

00:06:57.620 --> 00:07:01.980
of 130 mph in his Porsche 911 GT3.

00:07:01.980 --> 00:07:07.080
He seemingly lost control of the vehicle at
some point and collided violently with a tree.

00:07:07.080 --> 00:07:12.020
Upon collision, the car caught fire, and both
he and Hartwell were killed in the accident.

00:07:12.020 --> 00:07:17.520
The cause of death was attributed to a combination
of the impact and the subsequent fire.

00:07:17.520 --> 00:07:22.010
In addition to the fact that he was coming
from a bar and that several social media posts

00:07:22.010 --> 00:07:27.800
showed him drinking, a toxicology report confirmed
Dunn's intoxication at the time of the crash

00:07:27.800 --> 00:07:31.020
with a blood alcohol level around twice the
legal limit.

00:07:32.020 --> 00:07:36.640
Zachary Hartwell had known Ryan Dunn and Bam
Margera for several years.

00:07:36.640 --> 00:07:41.430
In addition to PA-ing on "Jackass Number Two,"
he'd done stunt driving on the film "Minghags,"

00:07:41.430 --> 00:07:45.400
which was written and directed by Margera
and starred Margera and Dunn.

00:07:45.400 --> 00:07:47.570
Additionally, Hartwell was an Iraq War vet.

00:07:47.570 --> 00:07:51.710
He served two tours overseas as a Navy gunner
before returning to America.

00:07:51.710 --> 00:07:55.449
In 2010, Hartwell married Rachel, his high
school sweetheart.

00:07:55.449 --> 00:07:59.389
They were married less than a year when Hartwell
died in the car crash.

00:07:59.389 --> 00:08:03.449
In August 2012, Hartwell's parents went to
court seeking damages.

00:08:03.449 --> 00:08:08.580
They named Dunn's estate — as well as Barnaby's,
the bar where Dunn was drinking — as defendants

00:08:08.580 --> 00:08:09.580
in the suit.

00:08:09.580 --> 00:08:14.230
In addition to wrongful death, the lawsuit
alleged the parties behaved negligently and

00:08:14.230 --> 00:08:18.070
recklessly and that those actions led to the
death of their son.

00:08:19.160 --> 00:08:23.640
Despite the tragic consequences of his actions,
Dunn's death left many bereft.

00:08:23.640 --> 00:08:27.730
After all, the man was beloved by many — both
people in his personal life and those who

00:08:27.730 --> 00:08:29.720
followed his adventures on the screen.

00:08:29.720 --> 00:08:36.050
As a result, fans and friends met the news
of his death with an outpouring of social media posts.

00:08:36.050 --> 00:08:41.700
Musical artists like Kings of Leon and Skrillex
dedicated songs from their concerts to Dunn's

00:08:41.700 --> 00:08:44.160
memory in the days after his demise.

00:08:44.160 --> 00:08:48.339
Singer-songwriter Roger Alan Wade even wrote
an original piece for him, which played at

00:08:48.339 --> 00:08:51.110
Dunn's memorial service in a special tribute
video.

00:08:51.110 --> 00:08:56.240
In 2013, the film "Jackass Presents: Bad Grandpa"
hit theaters everywhere.

00:08:56.240 --> 00:09:01.660
It was the first big-screen project from the
Jackass crew since 2011, and Dunn's co-workers

00:09:01.660 --> 00:09:04.660
and friends dedicated the project to their
late friend.

00:09:04.660 --> 00:09:09.450
In commemorative and anniversary releases,
the group has continued to feature Dunn in

00:09:09.450 --> 00:09:15.130
archival footage, and despite his tragic passing,
the man's legend still lives on through his

00:09:15.130 --> 00:09:17.580
crazy work in the world of "Jackass."

